@page "/docs/get-started/webassembly-and-auto"

@* Setting up an app with WebAssembly or Auto pages. The server project's steps are the Blazor Web
   App article's; this one adds the .Client project and what changes in the browser. *@

<ArticlePage Section="get-started" Slug="webassembly-and-auto">
    <Lead>
        WebAssembly and Auto pages run their components in the browser, from a <Code>.Client</Code> project that can't use
        ASP.NET Core's server framework. That project gets <Code>IzzyBlazor.Core</Code>, and the server project keeps
        <Code>IzzyBlazor</Code>.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Create the app</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="bash">dotnet new blazor --empty --interactivity Auto -o MyApp</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Use <Code>--interactivity WebAssembly</Code> for pages that only ever run in the browser. Either way you get two
            projects: <Code>MyApp</Code>, the server, and <Code>MyApp.Client</Code>, where interactive pages and components live.
            Pages are still static by default; a page runs in the browser once it has a render mode.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Set up the server project</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Follow the <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/blazor-web-app">Blazor Web App</Anchor> steps in <Code>MyApp</Code>: install
            <Code>IzzyBlazor</Code>, add the stylesheet and <Code>IzzyTheme</Code> to <Code>App.razor</Code>, add
            <Code>@@using IzzyBlazor</Code> to <Code>_Imports.razor</Code>, and register the services:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="MyApp/Program.cs" HighlightLines="2,13">@ServerProgram</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>App.razor</Code> always renders on the server, so the stylesheet and theme are set up once, there, for every
            page.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Set up the .Client project</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Install <Code>IzzyBlazor.Core</Code>:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">dotnet add MyApp.Client package IzzyBlazor.Core --prerelease</Code>
        <TextBlock>Import the components in <Code>MyApp.Client/_Imports.razor</Code>:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="MyApp.Client/_Imports.razor">@@using IzzyBlazor</Code>
        <TextBlock>And register its services:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="MyApp.Client/Program.cs" HighlightLines="4">@ClientProgram</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            An Auto page renders on the server first and in the browser later, so it uses both registrations.
            <Code>AddIzzyBlazor</Code> on the server includes everything <Code>AddIzzyBlazorCore</Code> adds.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Packages</Heading>
        <TextBlock>IzzyBlazor ships as two packages. Keep them on the same version.</TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Packages" Caption="Packages" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <TemplateColumn Title="Package" Width="Length.Rem(10)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
            <TemplateColumn Title="Contains">@context!.Contains</TemplateColumn>
            <TemplateColumn Title="Install in">@context!.Where</TemplateColumn>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            Every component is in <Code>IzzyBlazor.Core</Code>, and both packages use the <Code>IzzyBlazor</Code> namespace, so
            the same markup works in either project.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">In the browser</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Components behave the same in the browser, apart from what depends on the request:</TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Keep prerendering on</strong> (the default). <Anchor Href="docs/components/app-sidebar">AppSidebar</Anchor>'s
                remembered state, a dismissed <Anchor Href="docs/components/banner">Banner</Anchor> and the choice in
                <Anchor Href="docs/components/color-scheme-switch">ColorSchemeSwitch</Anchor> are read from cookies on the server and
                handed to the browser with the prerendered page. With <Code>prerender: false</Code> they start from their defaults.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Toasts show straight away.</strong> A redirect in the browser is a navigation, not a new request, so
                there's nothing to carry over. <Anchor Href="docs/components/toast-region">ToastRegion</Anchor> stays in the layout,
                which can be static.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Dialogs opened from code need a host in the browser.</strong> Put one <Code>&lt;DialogHost /&gt;</Code> on the
                interactive page, or in the layout with a render mode on the host
                (<Code>&lt;DialogHost @@rendermode="InteractiveAuto" /&gt;</Code>). See
                <Anchor Href="docs/components/dialog">Dialog</Anchor>.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components">Components</Anchor>: every component, with what it does in each mode.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor>: one form that posts on static pages and validates on interactive ones.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/get-started/existing-app">Add to an existing app</Anchor>: Bootstrap, your own components and name clashes.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Package(string Name, string Contains, string Where);

    private static readonly Package[] Packages =
    [
        new("IzzyBlazor", "Everything in IzzyBlazor.Core, plus the server pieces that read and write the request's cookies", "The server project"),
        new("IzzyBlazor.Core", "Every component, the CSS and the script, with no dependency on the ASP.NET Core server", "The .Client project, or a Razor class library"),
    ];

    private const string ServerProgram = """
        var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
        builder.Services.AddIzzyBlazor();
        builder.Services.AddRazorComponents()
            .AddInteractiveServerComponents()
            .AddInteractiveWebAssemblyComponents();

        var app = builder.Build();
        // ...
        app.MapRazorComponents<App>()
            .AddInteractiveServerRenderMode()
            .AddInteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode()
            .AddAdditionalAssemblies(typeof(MyApp.Client._Imports).Assembly);
        app.MapIzzyBlazor();

        app.Run();
        """;

    private const string ClientProgram = """
        using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Hosting;

        var builder = WebAssemblyHostBuilder.CreateDefault(args);
        builder.Services.AddIzzyBlazorCore();

        await builder.Build().RunAsync();
        """;
}
